{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -f cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,80p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Register lookahead EX forwarding decisions\n\n- Added two 2-bit forwarding choices to the ID/EX payload. The existing\n  ID/EX register now captures, holds, and flushes them with the instruction.\n- Reused `forward_unit` in ID to compare IF/ID source addresses with the\n  current ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations. EX consumes only the registered\n  choices; EX/MEM keeps priority over MEM/WB and x0 is excluded.\n- Used the effective EX and MEM write enables for lookahead. Misaligned\n  JAL/JALR targets and misaligned loads suppress their destination writes,\n  so younger instructions must not forward those results.\n- Left MEM/WB stall data retention unchanged. Directed tests cover both\n  operands, dual matches, load-use, redirects, divider holds, dmem stalls,\n  x0, and trapping producers.\n\nDeviation: the two effective producer write enables are exposed by EX and\nMEM stages to handle misalignment traps accurately.\n\nLocal checks: Verilator lint passed; pipeline cocotb suite passed; formal\npassed 53/53 checks on both runs with no fix attempts; cosim passed\nselftest and CoreMark with `NRET=1` (the bench core declares `nret: 1`).\n\nTiming: Gowin placement option 0 reports 52.213 MHz. The fixture metadata\nlists 50.274 MHz for the previous implementation. The remaining worst path\nruns from the register file to a dmem address input. The evaluator's median\nacross placements and CoreMark score are still pending.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
