{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_18", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,130p' formal/checks_si.cfg; sed -n '1,90p' formal/run_all.sh\"", "aggregated_output": "[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Run riscv-formal checks against the current $RTL_DIR/*.sv.\n# Requires: formal/riscv-formal cloned (manual clone or `git submodule init`).\n#\n# Stages every $RTL_DIR/*.sv plus wrapper.sv + the chosen checks config under\n# the riscv-formal tree, then runs sby -> bitwuzla via the framework's\n# generated makefile. Tallies PASS/FAIL by inspecting each task's\n# logfile.txt.\n#\n# Usage: bash formal/run_all.sh [checks-cfg-path]\n#   default: formal/checks.cfg       (fast, ALTOPS, used by orchestrator)\n#   deep   : formal/checks-deep.cfg  (no ALTOPS, proves M-ext arithmetic)\n#\n# Env var\n…[2514 of 5586 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:659a9dcad5eb4cd9]…\nelow\n# only removes dirs whose lock nobody holds; flock works across users and\n# PID namespaces, unlike the old `kill -0 <pid>` test (from inside a\n# sandbox every outside PID looks dead). Without flock(1), nothing is reaped.\nHAVE_FLOCK=\nif command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    exec 9>\"$CORE_DIR/.owner.lock\"\n    flock 9\n    HAVE_FLOCK=1\nfi\n\n# Auto-detect single-issue cores from core.yaml when WRAPPER + CHECKS_CFG\n# are both unset and $1 (checks-cfg-path) wasn't given either. Explicit\n# env vars or $1 still win. Uses python3 to parse YAML robustly — bash\n# grep would mis-handle quoted/indented variants and break on the next\n# yaml schema bump.\nCORE_YAML=\"$PROJECT_ROOT/cores/$CORE_NAME/core.yaml\"\nif [ -z \"${WRAPPER:-}\" ] && [ -z \"${CHECKS_CFG:-}\" ] && [ -z \"${1:-}\" ] \\\n   && [ -f \"$CORE_YAML\" ]; then\n    # Pass the path via env var so the python literal stays free of any\n    # bash-substituted strings that could break on quoting / spaces.\n    NRET=$(CORE_YAML=\"$CORE_YAML\" python3 -c '\nimport os, yaml\ntry:\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
