{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -f cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && sed -n '1,12p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Registered branch recovery\n\nEX now records correction-valid, correction PC, and conditional branch training metadata in EX/MEM. Fetch and hazard control consume only this registered correction. When it is asserted, ID/EX is flushed, the younger EX result is suppressed at EX/MEM, and a wrong-path divider cannot launch. Correction overrides instruction and data holds. The branch predictor trains once when its EX/MEM payload advances.\n\nThe existing 32-entry direction predictor, misaligned-target trap behavior, and single-channel RVFI retirement path remain in place. I kept the fetch PC update in `if_stage.sv` and routed the registered EX/MEM fields through `core.sv`; no extra pipeline stage or top-level port change was needed.\n\nAdded a pipeline test that checks a mispredicted branch during an instruction stall squashes younger store/load operations, preserves memory contents, and retires at the expected PCs and RVFI orders.\n\nValidation: Verilator lint passed; cocotb suite passed (5 pytest runners, including the new pipeline case); `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` passed 53/53 checks on the first attempt; `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and CoreMark. `make timing TARGET=bench` reported 47.189 MHz for placement option 0, with the reported worst path through the EX ALU to data memory. `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` passed CoreMark validation and reported median Fmax 49.251 MHz, 4,272,783 bracketed cycles, and fitness 115.27. The plain `make cosim`/`make fpga` commands select the harness default NRET=2 and cannot build this NRET=1 core locally, so local runs explicitly set `NRET=1`.\n\nConcern: moving correction one cycle later adds a bubble for each actual misprediction or JALR recovery. The measured local fitness exceeds the stated 112.36 baseline, but placement and stall sequences can vary in the orchestrator run.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
