{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short -- cores/bench && if [ -f cores/bench/LESSONS.md ]; then cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; fi'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 (improvement, +653.30%): Serializing RV32M DIV/REM into a shared 32-step unit removes combinational divide from the forwarded-operand EX path, so the clock-speed gain can outweigh divide stalls in CoreMark.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (regression, +458.50%): Unrolling four 33-bit restoring-divider steps per clock adds a long register-to-register path, while CoreMark's timed kernels contain no DIV/REM to benefit from the shorter divide latency.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (improvement, +23.07%): A 32-entry two-bit branch table with IF-computed direct targets saves CoreMark branch and JAL flush cycles while carrying only one prediction bit into EX, avoiding a wide predicted-PC comparison on the recovery path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 (improvement, +4.72%): Registering EX correction in EX/MEM and redirecting IF from MEM via pc_next breaks the register-file-to-EX-to-IF-to-ID critical loop, so the clock gain outweighs an extra recovery cycle when CoreMark branch misses and JALRs are sparse.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r4s1 (improvement, +9.82%): For CoreMark's frequent loads and stores, a dedicated EX effective-address adder feeding its own EX/MEM register bypasses the general ALU result mux on the forwarding-to-data-memory path without adding memory cycles.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r5s0 (improvement, +17.17%): Registering RV32M multiply variants before MEM and forwarding selection, then registering EX branch-training feedback before updating the IF direction table, cuts both DSP-to-EX/MEM mux delay and the EX-to-predictor feedback path without adding dependent-instruction bubbles.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 (regression, +5.49%): A two-entry predicted-path IF FIFO cuts CoreMark cycles from 4.27M to 4.06M by fetching through decode holds, but the EX forwarding and ALU critical path keeps Fmax near 55 MHz, leaving this IPC gain behind EX retiming.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r6s1 (improvement, +12.11%): Registering next-cycle forwarding selects and parallel EX result and PC candidates moves tag comparisons and wide result/target muxes off the EX critical path while preserving next-cycle dependent ALU forwarding.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r8s1 (regression, +3.59%): A four-entry predicted IF queue cuts CoreMark from 4.27M to 4.05M cycles, but a 1.7% Fmax loss on the EX-result-to-forward-select path limits its net gain, so deeper fetch buffering must repay its whole-core timing cost.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r8s2 (improvement, +5.53%): A tagged PC[5:2]-indexed replay table recovers hot-loop instructions during imem stalls without the 16-way fetch-to-decode search that cost Fmax, provided entries are filled only on accepted live fetches and invalidated on matching completed stores.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r9s1 (improvement, +10.56%): Computing registered forwarding selects speculatively from current pipeline tags removes MEM redirect and flush muxes from the correction-to-select timing path, since squashed ID/EX bubbles ignore those selects while data-memory stalls hold them with their payloads.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r10s0 (improvement, +7.13%): Registering per-source one-hot EX/MEM bank enables with pipeline payloads lets a masked-OR operand network fuse result selection with forwarding, removing a serial mux level from shared branch and multiplier paths.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
