{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -5 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Verify predicted branches from registered operands in MEM\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current predictor avoids most direct-branch redirects, but branch\\nverification still forms a long EX/MEM-register-to-forwarding-mux-to-branch-\\ncomparator-to-EX/MEM-register path. The latest champion's worst path ends\\nat correction_valid and limits Fmax to 72.531 MHz. Earlier predictor-table\\nenlargement reduced misses but lost timing, while fetch replay saved cycles\\nyet also lost timing. The best remaining predictor opportunity is to make\\nspeculation cheap to verify without adding a recovery cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current predictor avoids most direct-branch redirects, but branch\\nverification still forms a long EX/MEM-register-to-forwarding-mux-to-branch-\\ncomparator-to-EX/MEM-register path. The latest champion's worst path ends\\nat correction_valid and limits Fmax to 72.531 MHz. Earlier predictor-table\\nenlargement reduced misses but lost timing, while fetch replay saved cycles\\nyet also lost timing. The best remaining predictor opportunity is to make\\nspeculation cheap to verify without adding a recovery cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the existing 32-entry two-bit direction predictor and IF direct-target\\nprediction. Pass the predicted-taken bit into EX/MEM with the already\\nregistered forwarded rs1/rs2 values and PC candidates. In MEM, compare\\nthose registered operands, determine the actual branch outcome, and compare\\nit with the prediction. Drive the IF redirect and younger-stage squashes\\nfrom this MEM result in the same cycle that the present registered\\ncorrection redirects fetch. Select the architectural next PC and branch\\ntarget trap from the same resolved outcome bef