{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20260929-001-r8s0','hyp-20260929-001-r9s0','hyp-20260929-001-r10s0'):\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],'\\\\n',d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'] if isinstance(d.get('hypothesis'),dict) else d.get('hypothesis'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n hyp-20260929-001-r8s0 Share one signedness-configurable RV32M multiplier \n Replace the signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned EX\nmultipliers with one signed 33-by-33 multiplier. Extend each 32-bit input\nby either its sign bit or zero according to the decoded multiply variant:\nboth signed for MULH, only rs1 signed for MULHSU, and both unsigned for\nMULHU and MUL. Register the complete product at the EX/MEM boundary, and\nchoose its low or high 32 bits after that boundary for forwarding and\nwriteback. This preserves one-cycle dependent MUL issue while eliminating\nduplicate multiplier structures and product registers. Keep the existing\niterative divider and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitute behavior. Reduced DSP,\nregister, and routing pressure should lift Fmax by more than the 4.6%\nacceptance margin at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r9s0 Remove redirect control from registered forwarding selects \n Compute next-cycle forwarding selects from the source and destination tags\nthat will be held or captured on a normal advance, without qualifying tags\nor write enables by MEM correction or decode bubble injection. Use the raw\ndata-memory wait condition for held EX/MEM and MEM/WB tags, and expose a\ndivide pending condition for held ID/EX tags that is independent of MEM\ncorrection. Keep the real pipeline flushes, divider protocol, and stall\npriorities unchanged. A killed ID/EX or EX/MEM instruction has valid=0,\nso an arbitrary select alongside that invalid payload cannot change\narchitectural state; on the following live cycle the selectors are\nrecomputed from the correct registered tags. Retain EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB\nforwarding priority. Removing correction from the selector feedback cone\nshould raise Fmax without changing CoreMark cycles.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r10s0 Fuse EX/MEM result selection with operand forwarding \n While a producer is in ID/EX and its consumer is in fetch/decode, compute\neach consumer source's EX/MEM forwarding match as today and combine it with\nthe producer's ALU operation or jump-link choice. Register one mutually\nexclusive result-bank enable vector per source at the same edge as ID/EX\nand EX/MEM. In EX, form each operand with a balanced masked-OR network of\nthe enabled EX/MEM banks, the MEM/WB value, and the held ID/EX value. This\nfuses result-bank selection and EX/MEM forwarding into one data-selection\nnetwork, removing a serial 32-bit mux level from both the branch and DSP\npaths without changing issue latency. Keep the existing selected result\nfor MEM/WB and preserve jump alignment suppression, x0 exclusion, producer\npriority, formal ALTOPS values, divide bubbles, redirects, and data-bus\nstall alignment. The shared path reduction should lift Fmax beyond the\n4.6% champion acceptance margin at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
