{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-001-r2s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260929-001-r2s0\ntitle: \"Remove redirect feedback through fetch instruction and load-use hazard\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  After iterative division raised Fmax to 41.009 MHz, the worst timed path\n  crosses execute redirect, fetch, load-use hazard, and the ID register input.\n  The fetch stage currently substitutes a NOP for every redirect, so the\n  branch decision fans through a 32-bit instruction mux before source-register\n  comparisons and ID stall/flush control. This feedback is unnecessary because\n  the redirected fetch is already invalidated by a dedicated ID flush.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the fetched instruction bits independent of the EX redirect. Use the\n  existing valid bit and explicit redirect flush to kill a wrong-path fetch,\n  and qualify load-use detection with fetch validity so its arbitrary source\n  fields cannot request a stall. Retain NOP substitution for an unready imem\n  response, and preserve the current priority of data stalls, divide holds,\n  and redirects. This should shorten the register-to-register redirect and\n  decode control path without adding a pipeline stage or changing CoreMark IPC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Remove redirect from the instruction NOP mux while retaining redirect priority for PC update and clearing the fetch valid bit.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Qualify load-use detection with IF/ID validity and let redirect drive the existing ID kill directly, without influencing the fetched instruction bits.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire IF/ID validity into the hazard unit and preserve all existing memory-stall and divide-stall controls.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover taken branches with apparent wrong-path load-use matches and simultaneous instruction or data stalls, checking the retirement sequence and memory effects.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
