{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20260929-002-r10s1','hyp-20260929-002-r5s1','hyp-20260929-001-r11s0'):\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],'\\\\n',d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],'\\\\n',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n hyp-20260929-002-r5s1 Bank EX functional results and select after registration \n Partition EX into parallel result banks for integer arithmetic and logic,\nshifts and comparisons, raw RV32M products, the completed divide result,\nand jump link values. Register the banks at the EX/MEM boundary with a\ndecoded result selector. Choose the architectural value from those\nregistered banks for EX/MEM forwarding and MEM/WB writeback, including the\ncorrect high or low multiplier half. This moves the wide operation and\njump-result mux after the EX registers without changing ordinary issue or\nretirement cycles. Keep the dedicated effective-address register, iterative\ndivider and its ALTOPS behavior, forwarding priority, EX/MEM holds and\nbubbles, traps, and single-channel RVFI unchanged. The shorter EX path\nshould exceed the 4.6% acceptance margin despite an added forwarding mux.\n \n \n\n hyp-20260929-002-r10s1 Isolate rare M results from the immediate forwarding network \n Split EX/MEM result selection into an immediate-bypass lane for ordinary\nALU operations, link values, and low MUL, plus a complete result lane for\nMEM/WB writeback. Exclude MULH, MULHU, MULHSU, DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU\nfrom the immediate-bypass lane. Treat a valid high-multiply or divide in\nID/EX like a load-use producer: if the following instruction reads its\nnonzero destination, hold fetch and inject one ID/EX bubble. The consumer\nthen uses the full result from MEM/WB forwarding. Preserve EX/MEM priority,\nx0 behavior, bus-stall holds, redirects, and the existing divider protocol.\nThis structurally removes rare M-result selection from the timed\nEX/MEM-to-EX branch and ALU loops while leaving common MUL dependencies\nsingle-cycle and architectural retirement unchanged.\n \n \n\n hyp-20260929-001-r11s0 Register the selected writeback value before EX forwarding \n Select the final register write value in MEM and store one 32-bit result\nin MEM/WB, replacing the separately registered ALU and load values. Drive\nboth regfile writeback and MEM/WB-to-EX forwarding directly from that\nregister. This moves the load-versus-ALU mux across the MEM/WB boundary,\nremoves its control and data mux from the DSP and branch feedback paths,\nand saves one 32-bit result bank without changing latency. Preserve the\nexisting hold_wb behavior: a data-bus stall clears only valid and retains\nthe selected writeback value for a held dependent EX instruction. Keep\nmemory misalignment write suppression, x0 handling, ALTOPS, and all RVFI\ndata, trap, and retirement behavior intact. Expect enough Fmax gain to\nexceed the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold at the same CoreMark cycles.\n \n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
