{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r6s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260929-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Retimed execute results and control targets\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 21.038 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the general EX\n  ALU before reaching an EX/MEM register. A prior structural trial registered\n  parallel functional results and improved that path, but its new worst path\n  ended at the EX/MEM resolved next-PC register; its 145.6 fitness remained\n  below the 151.41 champion. Both paths contain wide selection before the\n  EX/MEM boundary. CoreMark needs the existing one-cycle ALU forwarding and\n  direct-branch prediction, so adding a general execute stage would be costly.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retopologize the EX/MEM boundary so it captures parallel arithmetic/logic,\n  shift, multiply, divide, and jump-link result banks plus narrow operation\n  control, then selects the architectural result from registered banks for\n  forwarding and MEM/WB. At the same boundary, capture the direct branch or\n  JAL target already computed during IF, the sequential PC, and an independent\n  JALR sum; register only narrow correction and target-select controls. Select\n  the resolved next PC and redirect target after those registers. This removes\n  both the general ALU result mux and the branch-condition/target mux from the\n  long EX register-input paths without adding an issue or memory cycle.\n  Preserve predictor behavior, JALR bit-zero clearing, misaligned-target\n  traps, RVFI pc_wdata, forwarding priority, divider holds, memory stalls,\n  redirect squashes, and single-channel retirement. Expect a clock gain above\n  the 4.6% acceptance margin with essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Carry registered EX result banks and separate direct, sequential, and JALR PC candidates with narrow selectors through EX/MEM.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Pass the existing IF-computed direct target and sequential PC with each fetched instruction while retaining the predictor and redirect priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture IF PC candidates in ID/EX alongside their instruction and prediction bit, preserving holds and flushes.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Register parallel result banks and independent next-PC candidates; register correction and target selection without a wide pre-register result or next-PC mux.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Select the registered EX/MEM result for forwarding and the registered correction target for IF, preserving one-cycle dependent ALU issue.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Select the registered architectural result and resolved RVFI next PC for MEM/WB while keeping the dedicated memory address path.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover back-to-back ALU dependencies and predicted, mispredicted, JAL, JALR, and misaligned control flows under bus stalls and redirects.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
