{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r9s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260929-002-r9s1\ntitle: \"Decouple forwarding tags from pipeline squash control\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 179.13 with 3,821,957 measured CoreMark cycles and\n  68.462 MHz Fmax. Its reported worst path starts at the registered MEM branch\n  correction and ends at a registered forwarding select. In the current\n  pipeline, that correction passes through stall and flush priority, changes\n  the next ID/EX and EX/MEM destination tags and write enables, and then\n  passes through tag comparison and priority selection before the clock edge.\n  This control path is paid on every cycle although the correction only needs\n  to invalidate younger instructions. Breaking it could lift Fmax above the\n  4.6 percent acceptance threshold without changing CoreMark's instruction\n  count, fetch replay behavior, or branch recovery latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retopologize the forwarding network into two parts. At the clock edge,\n  register speculative tag matches and source priority for the instruction\n  entering EX against the instructions entering EX/MEM and MEM/WB. Compute\n  these matches from raw register numbers and decoded write intent, without\n  feeding MEM correction, divide bubbles, or load-use flush through the\n  five-bit comparator and priority network. In parallel, clock narrow\n  forwardable bits that reflect the actual post-flush EX/MEM and MEM/WB\n  occupants. At EX, accept a registered bypass match only when its matching\n  source is forwardable; otherwise use the younger valid source or the held\n  ID/EX register value. Preserve EX/MEM priority when both writers target\n  the same register and preserve the retained MEM/WB data used by a stalled\n  dependent instruction after a memory handshake delay. Redirect and stall\n  decisions should reach only these narrow validity registers, while all\n  architectural side effects remain qualified by the existing stage-valid\n  rules. The prediction is a shorter correction-to-register path with the\n  same single-issue IPC and exact RVFI retirement sequence.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Register raw forwarding tag matches independently of redirect and stall control; track actual forwardable bits with pipeline validity and retain MEM/WB forwarding across data-memory holds.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Select EX operands using registered tag matches qualified by the corresponding source's forwardable bit, retaining EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority and divide operand capture.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Exercise dependent ALU, load, store, branch, and divide instructions across redirects and simultaneous instruction/data stalls; check register values and exactly-once ordered RVFI retirement.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
