{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(l)\n if x['id'] in ('hyp-20260929-001-r7s0','hyp-20260929-002-r6s1','hyp-20260929-003-r8s2'):\n  print('\\\\n',x['id'],x['title'])\n  print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'] if isinstance(x['hypothesis'],dict) else x['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n hyp-20260929-002-r6s1 Retimed execute results and control targets\nRetopologize the EX/MEM boundary so it captures parallel arithmetic/logic,\nshift, multiply, divide, and jump-link result banks plus narrow operation\ncontrol, then selects the architectural result from registered banks for\nforwarding and MEM/WB. At the same boundary, capture the direct branch or\nJAL target already computed during IF, the sequential PC, and an independent\nJALR sum; register only narrow correction and target-select controls. Select\nthe resolved next PC and redirect target after those registers. This removes\nboth the general ALU result mux and the branch-condition/target mux from the\nlong EX register-input paths without adding an issue or memory cycle.\nPreserve predictor behavior, JALR bit-zero clearing, misaligned-target\ntraps, RVFI pc_wdata, forwarding priority, divider holds, memory stalls,\nredirect squashes, and single-channel retirement. Expect a clock gain above\nthe 4.6% acceptance margin with essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r7s0 Predecode the EX/MEM forwarding result selection\nDecode the EX/MEM result-bank choice from ID/EX control before the EX/MEM\nedge and register mutually exclusive selection enables alongside the\nresult banks. Select the registered banks for forwarding with parallel\nmasking and a balanced OR tree, including jump-link, multiply variants,\niterative division, and the formal ALTOPS result. Keep the existing\narchitectural result and PC behavior, and align the selection enables\nwith EX/MEM reset, redirect, data-memory hold, and divide bubbles.\nThis removes the five-bit opcode comparison and priority case mux from\nthe EX/MEM-to-branch-correction timing path without changing issue\ncycles. A shorter dependent-branch path should raise Fmax enough to\nexceed the 4.6% fitness acceptance margin at unchanged CoreMark IPC.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r8s2 Direct-mapped short-loop replay predictor\nReplace the associative recent-window search with a 16-entry direct-mapped\nreplay table indexed by fetch PC[5:2]. Each entry keeps an exact upper-PC\ntag, the fetched instruction word, and a valid bit. Fill an entry only when\na live imem response is actually accepted. When io_imemReady is low, use a\ntagged hit as the instruction response and let the existing branch\ndirection/target predictor and decode advance normally; on a miss, retain\nthe present stall behavior. Keep the live imem word as the fast zero-wait\npath. Do not require an all-instructions-present loop scan or a 16-way\ncomparator before decode. Invalidate the matching entry on a completed\ndata-memory store, and suppress same-cycle replay for a store that could\nmodify the requested instruction word. Preserve redirect priority, exact\ninstruction PC, one-time branch training, RVFI retirement, and all bus\nhandshake rules. The indexed lookup should recover most of the prior\nreplay IPC gain while leaving enough Fmax to clear the 4.6% fitness bar.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
