{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nids={'hyp-20260929-003-r4s2','hyp-20260929-003-r6s2','hyp-20260929-003-r8s2','hyp-20260929-003-r9s2'}\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ids:\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],'\\\\n',d['hypothesis']['motivation'],'\\\\n',d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],'\\\\n',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 Fold branch PC bits to reduce direction-table aliasing \n The current 32-entry PC-indexed direction table sees 55,556 misses among\n529,588 conditional branches in an architectural trace of the measured\nCoreMark interval. Each miss now incurs registered MEM-stage recovery.\nHot branches 128 bytes apart collide in the table; for example, the branch\nat PC 0x240c is always taken but suffers 3,348 misses when it shares a\ncounter. A 128-entry gshare trial increased misses to 61,633, so global\noutcome history is a poor fit for this workload.\n \n Replace the 32-entry direction table with 64 two-bit counters indexed by\na folded branch PC: ((PC >> 2) ^ (PC >> 7) ^ (PC >> 10)) & 6'h3f. Apply\nthe identical hash to the EX-stage training PC, so the current one-bit\nprediction metadata remains unchanged. A software replay of the measured\nbranch trace reduces misses from 55,556 to 41,188, whereas a plain 64-entry\nindex still misses 51,846 times. This is one compact change to direction\nprediction: it separates hot branch PCs without adding global history,\ntarget RAM, or a wide predicted-PC comparison. Preserve valid-branch-only\ntraining, direct-target alignment checks, registered MEM correction, and\nexisting JAL/JALR treatment. The extra counter bits are outside the\ncurrently reported EX-to-dmem worst path; the reduced correction traffic\nshould improve iterations per cycle if placement keeps Fmax near 50 MHz.\n \n \n\n hyp-20260929-003-r6s2 Replay predicted short loops through instruction-bus stalls \n The existing direction table predicts direct branches well, but IF still\nneeds a fresh instruction-bus handshake for every loop iteration. A trace\nof the canonical timed CoreMark run has 235,191 taken backward branches;\nseveral 5- to 13-instruction loops repeat tens of thousands of times.\nRemoving instruction-bus stalls altogether reduces the run from 4.27M to\n3.46M cycles. Prior two-entry fetch buffering saved only 0.21M cycles and\nlost Fmax; repeated short loops offer more stall coverage with a bounded\npredictor-lo\n…[3424 of 6496 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7096a6351bd1ab5f]…\nark cycles, but its search path reduced Fmax. More indexed capacity is\nthe clearest remaining predictor-side opportunity to recover stalled fetches.\n \n Grow the existing direct-mapped replay table from 16 to 64 entries, indexed\nby fetch PC[7:2], with an exact PC[31:8] tag and one valid bit per entry.\nKeep the live imem response as the zero-wait data path and use the indexed\nreplay word only when imem is stalled and its tag matches. Organize the\nlarger table as four local 16-entry banks selected by PC[7:6], so each bank\nretains the existing narrow word lookup instead of introducing an\nassociative search. Fill only on accepted live fetches; invalidate the\nmatching bank and entry on a completed store, and block same-word replay\non that store cycle. Preserve redirect priority, branch prediction and\ntraining, bus handshake behavior, and ordered RVFI retirement. Capturing\nmore of CoreMark's recurring instruction footprint should remove enough\nimem-stall bubbles to outweigh the modest capacity and routing cost.\n \n \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
