{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 (improvement, +653.30%): Serializing RV32M DIV/REM into a shared 32-step unit removes combinational divide from the forwarded-operand EX path, so the clock-speed gain can outweigh divide stalls in CoreMark.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (regression, +458.50%): Unrolling four 33-bit restoring-divider steps per clock adds a long register-to-register path, while CoreMark's timed kernels contain no DIV/REM to benefit from the shorter divide latency.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (improvement, +23.07%): A 32-entry two-bit branch table with IF-computed direct targets saves CoreMark branch and JAL flush cycles while carrying only one prediction bit into EX, avoiding a wide predicted-PC comparison on the recovery path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 (improvement, +4.72%): Registering EX correction in EX/MEM and redirecting IF from MEM via pc_next breaks the register-file-to-EX-to-IF-to-ID critical loop, so the clock gain outweighs an extra recovery cycle when CoreMark branch misses and JALRs are sparse.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r4s1 (improvement, +9.82%): For CoreMark's frequent loads and stores, a dedicated EX effective-address adder feeding its own EX/MEM register bypasses the general ALU result mux on the forwarding-to-data-memory path without adding memory cycles.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r5s0 (improvement, +17.17%): Registering RV32M multiply variants before MEM and forwarding selection, then registering EX branch-training feedback before updating the IF direction table, cuts both DSP-to-EX/MEM mux delay and the EX-to-predictor feedback path without adding dependent-instruction bubbles.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 (regression, +5.49%): A two-entry predicted-path IF FIFO cuts CoreMark cycles from 4.27M to 4.06M by fetching through decode holds