{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('id') in ('hyp-20260929-003-r4s2','hyp-20260929-003-r10s2','hyp-20260929-003-r7s2'):\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'))\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','')[:2200])\n  print('notes',str(d.get('implementation_notes',''))[:500])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('id') in ('hyp-20260929-003-r4s2','hyp-20260929-003-r10s2','hyp-20260929-003-r7s2'):\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'))\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','')[:2200])\n  print('notes',str(d.get('implementation_notes',''))[:500])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 Fold branch PC bits to reduce direction-table aliasing fitness 117.08 cycles 4243344\nReplace the 32-entry direction table with 64 two-bit counters indexed by\na folded branch PC: ((PC >> 2) ^ (PC >> 7) ^ (PC >> 10)) & 6'h3f. Apply\nthe identical hash to the EX-stage training PC, so the current one-bit\nprediction metadata remains unchanged. A software replay of the measured\nbranch trace reduces misses from 55,556 to 41,188, whereas a plain 64-entry\nindex still misses 51,846 times. This is one compact change to direction\nprediction: it separates hot branch PCs without adding global history,\ntarget RAM, or a wide predicted-PC comparison. Preserve valid-branch-only\ntraining, direct-target alignment checks, registered MEM correction, and\nexisting JAL/JALR treatment. The extra counter bits are outside the\ncurrently reported EX-to-dmem worst path; the reduced correction traffic\nshould improve iterations per cycle if placement keeps Fmax near 50 MHz.\n\nnotes \n\n hyp-20260929-003-r7s2 Verify predicted branches from registered operands in MEM fitness 171.63 cycles 4272783\nKeep the existing 32-entry two-bit direction predictor and IF direct-target\nprediction. Pass the predicted-taken bit into EX/MEM with the already\nregistered forwarded rs1/rs2 values and PC candidates. In MEM, compare\nthose registered operands, determine the actual branch outcome, and compare\nit with the prediction. Drive the IF redirect and younger-stage squashes\nfrom this MEM result in the same cycle that the present registered\ncorrection redirects fetch. Select the architectural next PC and branch\ntarget trap from the same resolved outcome before MEM/WB captures RVFI.\nRetain EX handling of JALR target formation and its target-alignment trap;\nunconditional jumps can use the existing correction rule. Train each valid\nconditional branch once from its MEM outcome, suppressing a duplicate on\npipeline holds. This removes the forwarded-operand branch comparator from\nthe EX register-input timing path while preserving the current prediction\npolicy, recovery latency, retirement order, and bus-stall behavior. The\nexpected Fmax gain should outweigh the modest MEM-side comparison cost.\n\nnotes \n\n hyp-20260929-003-r10s2 Train branch direction from registered EX/MEM resolution fitness 195.43 cycles 3821957\nTrain the 32-entry two-bit direction table directly from each valid branch\nin EX/MEM: use its registered PC for the index and its registered pc_select\nto tell whether an aligned conditional branch was taken. Remove the\nseparate EX-to-IF training-valid, index, and outcome registers and the\ncorresponding EX output path. The EX/MEM register advances only when the\nbranch really issues; older MEM redirects squash it and data-memory stalls\nhold it behind the older operation. Thus each surviving branch trains once\nat the same edge as today's delayed feedback. A misaligned taken branch\nhas no usable predicted target and may train as not taken. Keep the existing\nIF prediction, correction latency, replay behavior, bus handshakes, traps,\nand single-channel RVFI retirement. Moving the feedback endpoint next to\nthe EX resolution logic should raise Fmax enough to exceed the 4.6 percent\nfitness acceptance margin with essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n\nnotes \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
