{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(s)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20260929-003-r2s2','hyp-20260929-003-r3s2','hyp-20260929-003-r4s2','hyp-20260929-003-r12s2']:\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],'\\\\n',d['motivation'],'\\\\n',d.get('implementation_notes','')[:1800])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 \n The current iterative-divider core reaches 41.009 MHz, but every taken\nbranch and JAL still redirects from EX and flushes a fetched instruction.\nThe prior 32-entry branch-history experiment reduced CoreMark cycles from\n4,491,485 to 4,203,502, showing useful control-flow locality; that attempt\nran on the old combinational-divider core, so its 5.366 MHz result does not\nmeasure the present design. Reusing the measured two-bit direction predictor\nwhile eliminating its 32-bit predicted-PC metadata targets the same cycle\nsavings with less register and correction logic on the current critical path.\n \n \n\n hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 \n The current predictor removes most taken-branch flushes, but its remaining\nEX correction signal feeds back through IF and hazard logic into ID/EX.\nGowin reports that exact register-file-to-EX-to-IF-to-ID loop as the\n27.579 ns critical path. A software model of its 32-entry counters over\nthe timed CoreMark instruction trace reports about 55,600 misses among\n529,600 conditional branches; the trace also has 13,700 JALRs in 4.2\nmillion measured cycles. Delaying their recovery by one cycle should cost\nroughly 1.7% IPC, leaving room for a useful Fmax gain.\n \n \n\n hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 \n The current 32-entry PC-indexed direction table sees 55,556 misses among\n529,588 conditional branches in an architectural trace of the measured\nCoreMark interval. Each miss now incurs registered MEM-stage recovery.\nHot branches 128 bytes apart collide in the table; for example, the branch\nat PC 0x240c is always taken but suffers 3,348 misses when it shares a\ncounter. A 128-entry gshare trial increased misses to 61,633, so global\noutcome history is a poor fit for this workload.\n \n \n\n hyp-20260929-003-r12s2 \n The champion's 16-word tagged replay table hides instruction-bus stalls in hot\nloops, but a hit still requests the same instruction from imem. Those ready\nbus cycles can fetch the predicted successor instead. Enlarging the replay\ntable to 64 or 256 words saved CoreMark cycles but reduced Gowin Fmax enough\nto regress, so a small change to fetch scheduling has a better area and\ntiming prospect than more storage.\n \n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
