{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'branch|JAL|jal|beq|bne|blt|bge|predict' cores/bench/test cores/bench/implementation_notes.md cores/bench/experiments 2>/dev/null | head -100\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py:39:async def i_type_jalr(dut):\ncores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py:40:    # JALR x1, 8(x1) = 0x008080E7 -> imm = 8\ncores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py:77:    # JAL x0, +8 = 0x0080006F -> imm = 8\ncores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py:79:    # JAL x0, -4 = 0xFFDFF06F -> imm = -4 = 0xFFFFFFFC\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Early direct control-flow prediction with a small branch history table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing IF stage always fetches PC+4 and waits for EX to resolve every\\nbranch and jump. Each taken direct branch or JAL therefore discards a fetch\\nslot. CoreMark repeatedly executes loop branches, and its cycle count is\\nlarge enough that removing those redirect bubbles should improve iterations\\nper cycle without changing the divider-dominated Fmax path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing IF stage always fetches PC+4 and waits for EX to resolve every\\nbranch and jump. Each taken direct branch or JAL therefore discards a fetch\\nslot. CoreMark repeatedly executes loop branches, and its cycle count is\\nlarge enough that removing those redirect bubbles should improve iterations\\nper cycle without changing the divider-dominated Fmax path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict aligned JAL targets in IF and predict validated conditional branches\\nusing a small PC-indexed table of two-bit saturating counters. Form direct\\ntargets from the current fetched instruction and PC. Carry the predicted\\nnext PC with the instruction into EX; redirect and flush only when the\\nresolved architectural next PC differs. Train the branch table exactly once\\nwhen a valid branch advances from EX: instruction stalls can coexist with\\ntraining, while data stalls delay it until EX advances. Keep JALR on the\\nsequential path because its target needs a register operand.\\nA 32-entry table should capture hot CoreMark loops with modest LUT/FF cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rt\n…[4398 of 7470 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:95742190414c2325]…\nbranch.value) == 1\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:196:        assert int(dut.branch_op.value) == op\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:201:async def reserved_branch_funct3(dut):\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:208:# ── JAL / JALR / LUI / AUIPC ──────────────────────────────────────────────\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:210:async def jal(dut):\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:211:    # JAL x0, +4 = 0x0040006F\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:219:async def jalr(dut):\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:220:    # JALR x1, 0(x2) = 0x000100E7\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:223:    assert int(dut.is_jalr.value)    == 1\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:228:async def jalr_nonzero_funct3_illegal(dut):\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:229:    # JALR with any funct3 != 0 is reserved per spec.\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:231:        instr = (f3 << 12) | 0x000000E7  # rd=1 rs1=0 imm=0 funct3=f3 opcode=JALR\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py:234:            f\"JALR funct3={f3} should be illegal (instr=0x{instr:08x})\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
