{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r6s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260929-003-r6s2\ntitle: \"Replay predicted short loops through instruction-bus stalls\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The existing direction table predicts direct branches well, but IF still\n  needs a fresh instruction-bus handshake for every loop iteration. A trace\n  of the canonical timed CoreMark run has 235,191 taken backward branches;\n  several 5- to 13-instruction loops repeat tens of thousands of times.\n  Removing instruction-bus stalls altogether reduces the run from 4.27M to\n  3.46M cycles. Prior two-entry fetch buffering saved only 0.21M cycles and\n  lost Fmax; repeated short loops offer more stall coverage with a bounded\n  predictor-local instruction stream.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one 16-instruction loop stream buffer beside the existing IF direction\n  predictor. Record instruction words and PCs only when a ready fetch is\n  actually consumed. When a valid taken backward branch resolves in EX,\n  arm replay only if its complete target-to-branch sequence is present as\n  contiguous, aligned PCs in the recent window. On later predicted passes,\n  serve matching PCs from the buffer when io_imemReady is low, letting ID\n  advance instead of inserting an instruction-stall bubble. Continue using\n  live bus data when ready, so first visits have the current zero-wait path.\n  A replay miss, branch exit, or registered MEM correction falls back to the\n  ordinary fetch path; correction has priority over replay and all holds.\n  Invalidate the window on a completed data-memory store to any captured\n  instruction word, so code writes cannot leave stale replay entries.\n  Register EX loop-arm metadata before IF consumes it, as current direction\n  training does, and keep replay selection out of the EX forwarding/ALU path.\n  This should hide a substantial share of the 0.82M instruction-stall cycles\n  without the continuously advancing request PC, two-entry FIFO arbitration,\n  or EX timing cost of the prior fetch-buffer experiment.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture the recent consumed fetch stream, confirm a short backward loop from registered EX metadata, and replay matching buffered instructions only during instruction-bus stalls; prioritize correction and invalidate on overlapping code stores.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Export the valid advancing branch PC and resolved backward target for loop qualification, suppressing updates on EX stalls, divide holds, and older MEM corrections.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect loop-arm and store-invalidation metadata and pass effective fetch availability to the hazard unit without changing top-level ports or retirement.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Treat a matching replay entry as an available instruction response while preserving load-use, data-bus, divide, and redirect priorities.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check short-loop replay across random instruction stalls, exits, branch mispredictions, overlapping code stores, data stalls, and exact RVFI instruction/PC order.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
